  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Tonight:\n\nSurface high pressure is building in today. Satellite shows a few \npockets of stratus lingering over northern MN and portions of NW WI \nwhere some snowfall is still being reported. Accumulations have\nbeen light and we expect more clearing skies as we head into \nthe late afternoon and evening hours as dry air works in from \nthe northwest. Expect another chilly start tomorrow morning with\nmorning low temperatures in the teens. \n\nRest of the work week:\n\nQuiet weather will remain in play through the work week and into \nThanksgiving. Surface high pressure will linger through tomorrow \nbefore sliding to the east as an upper level low advances southeast \nout of the Canadian Prairies and into the Central Plains. This low \nlacks a moisture source with low PWATs and model soundings showing a \nsubstantial dry slot in the low levels. As such we are not \nanticipating any precipitation with this system. \n\nTemperatures will gradually be on increasing through the week \nwith highs on Thanksgiving and Friday in the upper 30s to low \n40s. Overnight temperatures will consistently be below freezing.\n\nThe Weekend:\n\nOur next round of precipitation could arrive this weekend. The 12Z \nsuite of guidance is highlighting another low pressure moving across \nManitoba into Ontario. Most ensemble members keep this system north \nof the International Border with the latest AI models also inline \nwith this parameter spacing. However, run to run consistency remains \nlow. The current NBM is maintaining some low end PoPs of 20-40% \nspilling over into the region with the highest chances along the \nInternational Border. So for the time being additional snow \naccumulations remain very low through the weekend.",
